{"builder":"Goal: make packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js accept several files, and open one pull request that closes https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104.\n\nRight now packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js reads only process.argv[2] with readFileSync, prints `<file>:<line>: <error>` for each problem returned by lint(text) from packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js (newlines in the error become spaces), and exits 0, 1 or 2. Tests are in packages/jsonl-lint/test/cli.test.js (they spawn the bin with spawnSync) and packages/jsonl-lint/test/lint.test.js. The docs are in packages/jsonl-lint/README.md.\n\nSteps:\n1. Rewrite packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js so it takes `<file> [<file> ...]`:\n   - With no arguments, print a usage line (for example `usage: jsonl-lint <file> [<file> ...]`) to stderr and exit 2.\n   - If `-` appears more than once, it is a usage error: print a message to stderr and exit 2. Do this check before reading anything.\n   - Go through the files in the order given. For `-`, read stdin (for example readFileSync(0, 'utf8')) and use `-` as the name in the output. For any other argument, read the file, and use the argument exactly as typed in the output.\n   - If a file can't be read, print `jsonl-lint: cannot read <file>: <message>` to stderr, remember that it happened, and carry on with the remaining files. Do not exit early.\n   - For each file, print `<file>:<line>: <error>` to stdout for each bad row, in the same format as before, keeping the newline-to-space replacement.\n   - Exit code: 2 if any file couldn't be read. Otherwise 1 if any row in any file was bad. Otherwise 0.\n2. Do not change packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js. lint(text) must stay byte-for-byte the same (`git diff` of that file must be empty).\n3. Add node:test tests to packages/jsonl-lint/test/cli.test.js, using the existing `run` helper. For stdin, call spawnSync with the `input` option. Cover these cases:\n   - (a) two clean files: exit 0, no stdout;\n   - (b) one bad file among several: exit 1, and only the bad file's rows are printed, with the right `<file>:<line>:` prefixes in argument order;\n   - (c) `-` with clean stdin: exit 0;\n   - (d) `-` with bad stdin: exit 1, output lines start with `-:`;\n   - (e) a missing file among good ones: exit 2, a message on stderr, and a bad row in a later file is still reported on stdout;\n   - (f) two `-` arguments: exit 2;\n   - (g) no arguments: exit 2 with a usage message on stderr.\n   Keep the existing tests passing.\n4. Update packages/jsonl-lint/README.md. Usage becomes `node packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js <file> [<file> ...]`. Document that `-` reads stdin and is reported as `-`, that only one `-` is allowed, that files are checked in order, and the exit codes: 0 all clean; 1 any bad row; 2 no file, a second `-`, or any file unreadable, with other files still checked and reported.\n5. Run `npm test` at the repository root and make sure it passes.\n6. Commit, push, and open one PR on suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test. The PR body must contain the line `Closes https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104`. Wait for the `test` workflow (.github/workflows/test.yml) to finish on the PR head and make sure it is green. If it fails, fix the cause and push again.","verifier":"Check out the PR head, run `npm ci` (or `npm install`) at the repository root, and then run these checks in order. Each check is PASS or FAIL. Make temporary files in a scratch directory outside the repo. In the commands below, BIN is packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js.\n\nCheck 1. Expect line: \"packages/jsonl-lint/bin/jsonl-lint.js <file> [<file> ...] checks every file named in order and prints <file>:<line>: <error> for each bad row, as for one file before\".\nMake a.jsonl = `{\"a\":1}\\n{bad}\\n`, b.jsonl = `{\"b\":2}\\n`, c.jsonl = `{x}\\n{\"c\":3}\\n{y\\n`. Run `node BIN a.jsonl b.jsonl c.jsonl` and also `node BIN c.jsonl a.jsonl`.\nPASS: stdout has one line per bad row, each in the form `<file>:<line>: <error>`, using the file name exactly as given. That means a.jsonl:2, then c.jsonl:1 and c.jsonl:3 in the first run. In the second run the c.jsonl lines come before the a.jsonl line. For a single file, `node BIN a.jsonl` still prints `a.jsonl:2: <error>` as before.\nFAIL: a file is skipped, the order doesn't follow the argument order, a bad row is missing, or the line format is different.\n\nCheck 2. Expect line: \"A file named - is read from stdin and reported as -; a second - is a usage error (exit 2)\".\nRun `printf '{bad}\\n' | node BIN -` and `printf '{bad}\\n' | node BIN b.jsonl -`. Then run `printf '{}\\n' | node BIN - -`, and note its exit status.\nPASS: the stdin rows are checked and reported with `-:1: ...`, and the `- -` run exits 2.\nFAIL: stdin isn't read, it is reported under another name, or `- -` exits with anything other than 2.\n\nCheck 3. Expect line: \"Exit 0 if every file is clean, 1 if any row in any file is bad, 2 if no file is named or any file can't be read (error on stderr, other files still checked and reported)\".\nRun and note exit statuses for: `node BIN b.jsonl b.jsonl` (expect 0), `node BIN b.jsonl a.jsonl` (expect 1), `node BIN` (expect 2), and `node BIN b.jsonl missing.jsonl a.jsonl` (expect 2).\nPASS: every status matches. The no-argument run and the missing-file run write something to stderr. The missing-file run still prints `a.jsonl:2: ...` on stdout.\nFAIL: any status is different, stderr is empty in those two runs, or the files after the missing one aren't checked and reported.\n\nCheck 4. Expect line: \"lint(text) in src/index.js is unchanged\".\nRun `git diff <merge-base with main>..HEAD -- packages/jsonl-lint/src/index.js`.\nPASS: the lint function is unchanged. FAIL: the lint function's code or behaviour is changed.\n\nCheck 5. Expect line: \"node:test tests cover two clean files, one bad among several, stdin via - (clean and bad), a missing file among good ones, two - arguments and no arguments; the package README documents the usage\".\nRun `npm test` in packages/jsonl-lint and read the test files under packages/jsonl-lint/test/. Then read packages/jsonl-lint/README.md.\nPASS: there are node:test tests for each of the seven listed cases (two clean files; one bad among several; stdin via - clean; stdin via - bad; a missing file among good ones; two - arguments; no arguments), and they pass. The README documents the multi-file usage, including `-`.\nFAIL: any case has no test, the tests fail, or the README doesn't document the new usage.\n\nCheck 6. Expect line: \"The change is one PR on suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test whose body says Closes https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104, and npm test and the test workflow are green at its head\".\nRun `gh pr view <pr> --repo suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test --json body,headRefOid,state` and `gh pr checks <pr> --repo suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test`. Then run `npm test` at the repo root on the head.\nPASS: there is one PR, its body contains `Closes https://github.com/suredesigntshirts/ccp_machine_test/issues/104`, `npm test` passes locally, and the `test` workflow run for the head commit succeeded.\nFAIL: the line is missing from the body, the changes are split across more than one PR, `npm test` fails, or the workflow is failing or missing at the head.\n\nAdvisory (not pass/fail): it is nice if the second `-` is rejected before any input is read, if the usage message mentions `<file> [<file> ...]`, and if the unreadable-file message follows `jsonl-lint: cannot read <file>: ...`."}